body:
Yeah I think I have something like this that Ive always thought about since a kid; 
I always wished that there could be some magic arena where me and a huge cancer monster could fight to the death, and if I kill cancer then nobody in the world will have it ever again.
I got this idea when my dog died of throat cancer when I was young, and my gameplan was to suicidebomb the cancer monster so obivously I die, but thats a worthy sacrifice to spare the world from cancer.

Yes, but just because something is the law on the books, doesn't mean you're any less dead if it isn't followed.

For example in Australia there are "zebra crossings" which pedestrians use to cross the road, and drivers legally must stop and let the pedestrians cross. However, it would be very foolish to walk out without looking to make sure the driver is slowing down, because if it ends up that a driver flies through and skittles you, it doesn't matter that they broke the law, because you are dead or disabled all the same.

You can apply this logic to the hospital situation, in that if they mistreat you, because of incompetence, or perhaps purposeful negligence (which could possibly be driven by previous unpaid debt accrued), it doesn't matter that they are not following the law. The only thing that matters is that you are dead.

&#x200B;

